1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They are the largest moving objects ever built.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Their fleet carries over 100 million tons of oil

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:10,000
in a single day.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Targets in wars and targeted by environmentalists,

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
they are an essential lifeline to industrialize nations

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:19,000
around the globe.

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Now, oil tankers on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Longer than most skyscrapers are tall, they are steel giants

9
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:40,000
carrying Earth's most valuable commodity.

10
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:44,000
Oil tankers transport over half of the nearly 80 million barrels

11
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:49,000
of oil the world consumes on a daily basis.

12
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:53,000
In an oil-dependent world, no nation consumes more than the

13
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:59,000
United States. But America only produces about

14
00:00:59,001 --> 00:01:01,000
of its needed supply.

15
00:01:01,001 --> 00:01:06,000
The rest must be imported from thousands of miles away.

16
00:01:06,001 --> 00:01:10,000
Regretfully, oil is found in some locations that are a great

17
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:14,000
distance from markets such as the United States and Europe.

18
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:18,000
The only way to move that is either by ship or by pipeline.

19
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Oil tankers come in a range of sizes

20
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:30,240
and are classified by their DWT, or dead weight tonnage, which

21
00:01:30,241 --> 00:01:33,740
is a tanker's weight when fully loaded.

22
00:01:33,741 --> 00:01:39,140
The five dominant classes are named Panamax, Afromax, Suez

23
00:01:39,141 --> 00:01:45,320
Max, very large crude carriers, or VLCCs, and ultra-large crude

24
00:01:45,321 --> 00:01:48,360
carriers, or ULCCs.

25
00:01:48,361 --> 00:01:51,760
The latter two are more commonly referred to as supertankers.

26
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
In some cases, a tanker's classification name also specifies

27
00:01:59,001 --> 00:02:04,000
the region in which it can safely operate when fully loaded.

28
00:02:04,001 --> 00:02:08,000
For example, a Panamax is the largest tanker that can pass

29
00:02:08,001 --> 00:02:13,000
through the Panama Canal. A Suez Max is the maximum size

30
00:02:13,001 --> 00:02:17,000
tanker that is permitted to transit the Suez Canal.

31
00:02:17,001 --> 00:02:21,000
Larger tankers, such as super tankers, have drafts that are

32
00:02:21,001 --> 00:02:25,800
and must use alternative waterways.

33
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:28,840
Although oil tankers come in a variety of sizes,

34
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:34,340
they're all built for one singular purpose, to carry oil.

35
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:38,860
Lots and lots of oil. And this lone intent is reflected

36
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:44,560
in the tanker's design. Beneath the steel skin of an oil

37
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:49,200
tanker are a series of tanks subdivided by bulkheads.

38
00:02:49,201 --> 00:02:52,340
In a large-sized tanker, tanks are generally divided

39
00:02:52,341 --> 00:02:55,240
into three compartments that extend the width of the hull,

40
00:02:57,040 --> 00:03:00,660
and as many as 40 compartments that extend the length of the hull.

41
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:06,920
The reason for doing that is to prevent the cargo sloshing

42
00:03:06,921 --> 00:03:08,260
and to provide stability.

43
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:10,900
I think often the easy way of explaining it is

44
00:03:10,901 --> 00:03:13,780
if you were to carry a large bowl of water,

45
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:16,580
I think one becomes very conscious of the fact

46
00:03:16,581 --> 00:03:18,620
that it's difficult to maintain the stability.

47
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:22,300
If you can subdivide it by putting in divisions,

48
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:25,560
it makes it a much more stable cargo.

49
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:30,100
Aft of the cargo tanks at the stern of the ship

50
00:03:30,101 --> 00:03:33,020
is a lone tower known as the superstructure,

51
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:36,620
which houses all the necessary control rooms

52
00:03:36,621 --> 00:03:37,680
and crew accommodations.

53
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:42,460
The remainder of a tanker's deck

54
00:03:42,461 --> 00:03:45,860
is an array of pipes used to transport oil

55
00:03:45,861 --> 00:03:48,800
either into or out of the tank compartments below.

56
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:54,840
As soon as an oil tanker leaves port, it becomes an entirely

57
00:03:54,841 --> 00:03:59,880
self-sufficient floating city, capable of producing all of its

58
00:03:59,881 --> 00:04:04,440
own electricity. They have huge generating units

59
00:04:04,441 --> 00:04:09,280
and generating electricity to not only keep the cargo contained

60
00:04:09,281 --> 00:04:13,120
and heated, but also to heat the ship and to run the equipment.

61
00:04:13,121 --> 00:04:18,000
They're very large power plants moving across the ocean.

62
00:04:18,001 --> 00:04:21,300
And the larger the tanker, the more easily it can slip

63
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:22,700
through the water.

64
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:27,640
Therefore, a VLCC requires only a single 30,000 horsepower

65
00:04:27,740 --> 00:04:31,240
diesel engine for power.

66
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:34,080
The propulsion system consists of a single propeller

67
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:40,080
and rudder, capable of moving a fully loaded VLCC at a speed

68
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:42,660
of 15 knots.

69
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:46,160
Auxiliary generators produce the ship's electricity.

70
00:04:46,161 --> 00:04:51,160
Tankers, by nature, are the most energy efficient of all vehicles

71
00:04:51,260 --> 00:04:54,640
in terms of energy per ton mile of the goods transported.

72
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:58,640
We're moving it at a cost of about 2 cents per gallon.

73
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:02,200
So at the gas pump, the actual price we're paying for gasoline

74
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:06,640
has about 2 cents for having transported the oil.

75
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:09,120
At a port in the Pacific Northwest,

76
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:11,560
the Suez Max tanker British Harrier

77
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:13,560
is in the process of offloading cargo

78
00:05:13,561 --> 00:05:17,560
cargo to its Cherry Point refinery.

