00:05:17,561 --> 00:05:19,560
The crude we're carrying is from Argentina,

80
00:05:19,561 --> 00:05:21,560
and it's called Escalante.

81
00:05:21,561 --> 00:05:24,560
We carry 1 million barrels.

82
00:05:24,561 --> 00:05:30,560
It's that equivalent to probably 35 million gallons or so.

83
00:05:30,561 --> 00:05:32,560
It's a lot of oil.

84
00:05:32,561 --> 00:05:34,560
Although the Harrier looks safe

85
00:05:34,561 --> 00:05:36,560
in the calm waters of the port,

86
00:05:36,561 --> 00:05:39,560
offloading is actually one of the most dangerous aspects

87
00:05:39,561 --> 00:05:41,560
of its journey.

88
00:05:41,561 --> 00:05:43,360
Certainly, this is the risky part.

89
00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:47,300
We changed the condition of the vessel greatly.

90
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:53,300
This is dynamic process, and it requires careful monitoring

91
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:59,220
to avoid incidents, accidents, bad things, really.

92
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:02,140
During cargo discharge, combustible gases

93
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:04,320
are present on the deck of the ship.

94
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:07,320
A single spark can lead to a catastrophic explosion.

95
00:06:07,321 --> 00:06:13,220
explosion. Crude oil is offloaded by huge

96
00:06:13,221 --> 00:06:16,060
steel pipes that extend the length of a tanker's center

97
00:06:16,061 --> 00:06:20,700
compartments. Additional pipes branch out

98
00:06:20,701 --> 00:06:24,040
