The MAUMI was the first ship to perform underway

245
00:16:24,381 --> 00:16:27,220
replenishment. The advantage of

246
00:16:27,221 --> 00:16:30,980
underway replenishment is that the fleet or Navy formation

247
00:16:30,981 --> 00:16:34,980
formation could keep steaming while it is refueling as versus

248
00:16:34,981 --> 00:16:38,980
having to go to a port somewhere and spend the time to refuel and

249
00:16:38,981 --> 00:16:41,620
then proceed on to its destination.

250
00:16:41,621 --> 00:16:46,820
By July 5, 1917, the Maumi had refueled 34 destroyers

251
00:16:46,821 --> 00:16:50,740
headed for Europe. They helped break the German U-boat

252
00:16:50,741 --> 00:16:53,640
blockade. The significance of refueling

253
00:16:53,641 --> 00:16:58,180
these destroyers was it was the first time that anyone had

254
00:16:58,181 --> 00:17:02,420
refueled ships at sea in a tactical operation.

255
00:17:02,520 --> 00:17:06,420
And it was the forerunner of something that would happen

256
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:09,620
again and again, especially in World War II.

257
00:17:09,720 --> 00:17:13,200
In the mid-1930s, war clouds began to form

258
00:17:13,300 --> 00:17:15,700
again on the global landscape.

259
00:17:15,800 --> 00:17:19,260
The United States military was quick to realize that if a second

260
00:17:19,360 --> 00:17:22,500
world war were to erupt, it would be a fully mechanized

261
00:17:22,600 --> 00:17:24,840
conflict, and tremendous amounts of oil

262
00:17:24,940 --> 00:17:27,340
would be needed to ensure victory.

263
00:17:27,341 --> 00:17:30,840
The Navy laid out a very detailed structural plan

264
00:17:30,940 --> 00:17:35,280
on how the Navy was going to get from the west coast

265
00:17:35,380 --> 00:17:37,280
of the United States to the Far East

266
00:17:37,380 --> 00:17:41,380
to be able to engage its main enemy, which it felt was Japan.

267
00:17:41,480 --> 00:17:45,820
So it knew in the interwar years that refueling at sea

268
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:49,400
would become a critical element of this.

269
00:17:49,500 --> 00:17:52,040
The Navy needed a fleet oiler in a hurry

270
00:17:52,140 --> 00:17:54,360
and looked towards the commercial tanker industry

271
00:17:54,460 --> 00:17:56,260
for assistance.

272
00:17:56,261 --> 00:18:02,060
In 1939, the Navy purchased the 553-foot tanker Cimarron

273
00:18:02,061 --> 00:18:04,460
from the Standard Oil Company.

274
00:18:04,461 --> 00:18:06,460
She was a very unusual ship.

275
00:18:06,461 --> 00:18:10,460
She was fairly large, but more importantly, she had twin screws.

276
00:18:10,461 --> 00:18:13,060
It's a very fast ship with a bulbous bow.

277
00:18:13,061 --> 00:18:17,060
They allowed the ship to have a speed of 18 knots at a time when

278
00:18:17,061 --> 00:18:23,060
14 or 15 knots was the norm for a fast commercial tanker.

279
00:18:23,061 --> 00:18:25,460
And this speed was very important because this would allow the

280
00:18:25,461 --> 00:18:28,600
would allow the ship to steam with the fleet.

281
00:18:28,700 --> 00:18:30,800
The Cimarron became the prototype fleet

282
00:18:30,900 --> 00:18:32,000
oiler for the Navy.

283
00:18:38,100 --> 00:18:40,600
When the US entered the war after the bombing of Pearl

284
00:18:40,700 --> 00:18:44,020
Harbor, the Cimarron class quickly distinguished itself

285
00:18:44,120 --> 00:18:46,920
in the battle for the Pacific.

286
00:18:47,020 --> 00:18:49,760
The real significance of the Cimarron class

287
00:18:49,860 --> 00:18:51,920
was that it permitted the United States

288
00:18:51,921 --> 00:18:54,680
to conduct the famous carrier raids

289
00:18:54,780 --> 00:18:56,060
that we're all familiar with.

290
00:18:56,160 --> 00:19:00,460
It's a little known fact that every one of those raids

291
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:03,060
was accompanied by an oiler.

292
00:19:03,160 --> 00:19:05,460
And this was because the destroyers

293
00:19:05,560 --> 00:19:08,140
all had to be refueled at sea because they had

294
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:10,740
limited range vessels.

295
00:19:10,840 --> 00:19:12,900
In addition to refueling at sea,

296
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,780
the US military needed to transfer millions of gallons

297
00:19:15,880 --> 00:19:21,220
of oil to the thousands of tanks, trucks, and planes

298
00:19:21,221 --> 00:19:24,960
It would be needed in Europe and along the Pacific fronts.

299
00:19:25,060 --> 00:19:28,320
Tankers were always a prime target of U-boats

300
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:30,320
because they're important ships.

301
00:19:30,420 --> 00:19:34,260
Their cargo is important. So whenever a submarine saw a

302
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:38,200
tanker, it would always go after it.

303
00:19:38,300 --> 00:19:40,980
The Allied forces were faced with the challenge of building

304
00:19:41,080 --> 00:19:43,680
tankers faster than the Axis powers could sink them.

305
00:19:46,780 --> 00:19:50,180
Their answer was the T-2 tanker.

306
00:19:50,181 --> 00:19:54,500
It was a standard design which allowed the ships to be built very quickly.

307
00:19:55,100 --> 00:20:00,380
They actually would construct segments on shore and then bring them to the building yard.

308
00:20:00,740 --> 00:20:05,000
And each time they built a tanker, they learned how to build it faster or quicker.

309
00:20:06,020 --> 00:20:08,820
About 525 of them were built throughout the war.

310
00:20:09,100 --> 00:20:10,680
They could do about 15 knots.

311
00:20:11,220 --> 00:20:12,860
They could carry all forms of petroleum.

312
00:20:12,861 --> 00:20:18,060
They were very instrumental in being able to support our forces

313
00:20:18,061 --> 00:20:22,360
overseas with large amounts of petroleum products.

314
00:20:22,361 --> 00:20:25,200
By the end of the war, the Navy's oilers and tankers

315
00:20:25,201 --> 00:20:28,200
have provided an invaluable lifeline of fuel

316
00:20:28,201 --> 00:20:32,020
to the Allied forces.

317
00:20:32,021 --> 00:20:35,420
While the oil tanker of World War II had achieved new levels

318
00:20:35,421 --> 00:20:39,760
of performance and size, a booming post-war economy was

319
00:20:39,761 --> 00:20:43,360
to give rise to the oil tanker Tycoon and the dawn of the

320
00:20:43,361 --> 00:20:48,160
supertanker. During the invasion of Okinawa

321
00:20:48,161 --> 00:20:52,000
in World War II, oilers delivered over 10 million barrels

322
00:20:52,001 --> 00:20:56,400
of fuel oil to the fleet at sea by underweight replenishment.

323
00:21:00,400 --> 00:21:04,000
The end of World War II marked the beginning of unprecedented

324
00:21:04,001 --> 00:21:07,760
growth for the United States economy.

325
00:21:07,761 --> 00:21:11,760
Post-war Americans wanted to drive fast and travel far.

326
00:21:13,760 --> 00:21:17,760
By the early 1950s, American imports of petroleum products

327
00:21:17,761 --> 00:21:21,760
exceeded exports for the first time.

328
00:21:21,761 --> 00:21:25,760
Oil companies and ambitious entrepreneurs were eager to feed

329
00:21:25,761 --> 00:21:29,760
America's high demand for oil and took advantage of a unique

330
00:21:29,761 --> 00:21:32,760
opportunity. After the Second World War,

331
00:21:32,761 --> 00:21:36,760
the US government has over 600 tankers of all sorts.

332
00:21:36,761 --> 00:21:40,360
all sorts. The American oil companies put pressure

333
00:21:40,361 --> 00:21:44,260
upon the US government to sell off these surplus T2 tankers.

334
00:21:44,261 --> 00:21:48,640
So for about four years, the United States sells over 400

335
00:21:48,641 --> 00:21:54,880
of these 600 tankers to American and foreign oil companies.

336
00:21:54,881 --> 00:21:57,820
Many of the T2 tankers were retrofitted by their new

337
00:21:57,821 --> 00:22:02,720
owners so that they could carry more cargo.

338
00:22:02,721 --> 00:22:05,620
Using a process called jumboizing,

339
00:22:05,621 --> 00:22:09,460
the ships were cut in half, and an extra section was added to

340
00:22:09,461 --> 00:22:14,500
the middle, thus doubling the tanker's storage capacity.

341
00:22:14,501 --> 00:22:18,340
The 1950s also saw a trio of independent shipping tycoons

342
00:22:18,341 --> 00:22:21,000
begin to stake their claim in the burgeoning oil trade.

343
00:22:23,940 --> 00:22:27,400
Greek shipping magnates Aristotle Onassis and Stavros

344
00:22:27,401 --> 00:22:31,480
Nyarkos joined American Daniel Ludwig in an unofficial

345
00:22:31,481 --> 00:22:34,780
competition over who could lay claim to the title of owner of

346
00:22:34,781 --> 00:22:40,460
world's largest tanker. The competition that existed between

347
00:22:40,461 --> 00:22:46,540
the various independent owners was probably as much ego as it was economics of the day.

348
00:22:46,541 --> 00:22:50,620
There was a lot of status carrying, I now have the world's largest tanker.

349
00:22:51,820 --> 00:22:57,340
American ship owner Daniel Ludwig chose to go overseas to the Kure shipyards in Japan to build

350
00:22:57,341 --> 00:23:03,420
his tanker fleet. That was a surprise to the industry

351
00:23:03,421 --> 00:23:08,220
world wide, because historically the ships were either built in Europe or they were built

352
00:23:08,221 --> 00:23:12,920
in the United States. And all of a sudden, Japan came in as a third competitor.

353
00:23:14,420 --> 00:23:20,000
In Japan, Ludwig found a country desperate for industry and a highly skilled workforce

354
00:23:20,001 --> 00:23:26,480
with a proud tradition in shipbuilding. As Ludwig began to build his tanker fleet,

355
00:23:27,300 --> 00:23:31,820
Greek ship owner Aristotle Onassis grabbed the title of world's largest tanker owner.

356
00:23:31,821 --> 00:23:35,620
with the launching of the Tena Onassis in 1952.

357
00:23:37,720 --> 00:23:42,000
His Greek rival, Stavros Nyarkos, bested him in 1955

358
00:23:42,001 --> 00:23:47,300
with a 47,000 deadweight ton vessel, Spiros Nyarkos.

359
00:23:47,960 --> 00:23:50,760
But it was political instability in the Middle East

360
00:23:50,761 --> 00:23:53,800
that would bring tanker tonnage to unprecedented levels.

361
00:23:55,840 --> 00:23:59,760
The Suez Canal, which links the Mediterranean Sea

362
00:23:59,761 --> 00:24:00,580
with the Red Sea.

363
00:24:01,820 --> 00:24:05,160
provides the shortest shipping route from the oil-rich Middle

364
00:24:05,161 --> 00:24:07,820
East to ports in Europe and the United States.

365
00:24:11,020 --> 00:24:15,660
In 1956, Egypt nationalized the previously unrestricted

366
00:24:15,661 --> 00:24:18,580
passageway. Israel, which relied heavily

367
00:24:18,581 --> 00:24:22,700
on the canal for trade, considered this a threat.

368
00:24:22,701 --> 00:24:27,540
Joined by Britain and France, Israel declared war on Egypt.

369
00:24:27,541 --> 00:24:31,080
The conflict left ships scuttled along the vital waterway,

370
00:24:31,081 --> 00:24:35,080
was closed. Ultimately, the United Nations

371
00:24:35,081 --> 00:24:39,080
settled the conflict, and the canal was reopened.

372
00:24:39,081 --> 00:24:42,080
But the tanker industry felt it could no longer rely on the

373
00:24:42,081 --> 00:24:45,080
political instability of the passageway.

374
00:24:45,081 --> 00:24:48,080
There was a realization that the oil that was moving from

375
00:24:48,081 --> 00:24:52,080
the Middle East to Europe to the United States had to come

376
00:24:52,081 --> 00:24:56,080
around South Africa, around the Cape.

377
00:24:56,081 --> 00:24:59,080
The route around the Cape of Good Hope and then north to

378
00:24:59,081 --> 00:25:04,840
Europe or the US, added as much as 11,000 miles round trip to a

379
00:25:04,841 --> 00:25:11,240
tanker's voyage. To maintain the normal flow of

380
00:25:11,241 --> 00:25:15,240
crew to both continents, tanker tonnage had to increase by

381
00:25:15,241 --> 00:25:19,720
nearly 100%. The industry was left with two

382
00:25:19,721 --> 00:25:24,120
alternatives, either build a large fleet of small vessels or a

383
00:25:24,121 --> 00:25:29,060
small fleet of large vessels. The decision came down to the

384
00:25:29,061 --> 00:25:31,060
economics of scale.

385
00:25:31,160 --> 00:25:35,000
If you had a box 10 foot by 10 foot by 10 foot,

386
00:25:35,100 --> 00:25:37,500
there is a, let's call it feet.

387
00:25:37,600 --> 00:25:40,300
There's 1,000 feet within that box.

388
00:25:40,400 --> 00:25:45,040
If you go 20, 20, and 20, it is eight times the volume.

389
00:25:45,140 --> 00:25:46,540
So it's not linear.

390
00:25:46,640 --> 00:25:50,940
By just doubling the size, you create huge amount of volume.

391
00:25:51,040 --> 00:25:55,020
And there was a rush to build a lot of large tankers, super

392
00:25:55,120 --> 00:25:56,320
tankers at that time.

393
00:25:56,321 --> 00:26:01,320
A large part of the industry said this is the future.

394
00:26:01,420 --> 00:26:03,860
After the closure of the Suez Canal,

395
00:26:03,960 --> 00:26:06,560
Daniel Ludwig set the maritime world abuzz

396
00:26:06,660 --> 00:26:09,660
by breaking the 100,000 deadweight ton barrier

397
00:26:09,760 --> 00:26:12,860
with the launching of the universe Apollo.

398
00:26:12,960 --> 00:26:17,880
The supertanker was born. Around this same time,

399
00:26:17,980 --> 00:26:21,880
a dramatic change began to take place in tanker design.

400
00:26:21,980 --> 00:26:25,280
The three tower arrangement that originated with the glukhov

401
00:26:25,281 --> 00:26:29,580
was combined into one superstructure placed at the stern,

402
00:26:29,581 --> 00:26:33,880
leaving the entire forward hull for tank space.

403
00:26:33,881 --> 00:26:37,820
As maritime engineers began to transform the tanker's design,

404
00:26:37,821 --> 00:26:41,020
deadweight tonnage barriers continued to fall.

405
00:26:41,021 --> 00:26:45,540
In 1966, the Irimitsu Maru became the first tanker to eclipse

406
00:26:45,541 --> 00:26:50,740
200,000 deadweight tons, becoming the first VLCC, or

407
00:26:50,741 --> 00:26:54,040
Very Large Crude Carrier.

408
00:26:54,041 --> 00:26:58,780
Just one year later, the Suez Canal closed again, this time

409
00:26:58,781 --> 00:27:01,840
for eight years. The industry's gamble in building

410
00:27:01,841 --> 00:27:06,380
supertankers had paid off. And Daniel Ludwig was ready to

411
00:27:06,381 --> 00:27:10,800
push the envelope of size even farther.

412
00:27:10,801 --> 00:27:16,800
In 1967, he unveiled the 300,000 deadweight ton, Universe

413
00:27:16,801 --> 00:27:19,940
Ireland. Ludwig had once again established

414
00:27:19,941 --> 00:27:23,040
a new record in tanker tonnage.

415
00:27:23,140 --> 00:27:27,840
The Universe Ireland became the first ULCC, or Ultra Large

416
00:27:27,940 --> 00:27:31,180
Crew Carrier.

417
00:27:31,280 --> 00:27:35,380
Ludwig's landmark achievements, breaking the 100 and 300,000

418
00:27:35,480 --> 00:27:39,300
tonnage barriers, gave him the recognition among his peers as

419
00:27:39,400 --> 00:27:43,100
the father of the supertanker.

420
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:47,540
But for the United States, the world's largest importer of oil,

421
00:27:47,541 --> 00:27:53,040
the draft of a fully loaded supertanker posed a large problem.

422
00:27:53,041 --> 00:27:55,940
None of the ports could handle a ship of that draft.

423
00:27:55,941 --> 00:27:59,180
So what we find happening still today in the United States

424
00:27:59,181 --> 00:28:02,080
is these larger crude carriers come in.

425
00:28:02,081 --> 00:28:05,020
They stay offshore 20, 30 miles,

426
00:28:05,021 --> 00:28:07,600
and they actually do what we call lightering,

427
00:28:07,601 --> 00:28:10,820
which is take the oil from the large ship

428
00:28:10,821 --> 00:28:14,040
and actually put it on a smaller ship, which does have the draft

429
00:28:14,041 --> 00:28:16,660
to allow it to go into these ports.

430
00:28:16,661 --> 00:28:19,200
Lightering typically takes place while both ships are

431
00:28:19,300 --> 00:28:20,900
underway.

432
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:24,200
Maneuvering the smaller ship alongside the larger tanker

433
00:28:24,300 --> 00:28:27,200
can be dangerous.

434
00:28:27,300 --> 00:28:29,800
This is a lightering fender.

435
00:28:29,900 --> 00:28:34,520
What we use them for is to keep the two ships that are lightering

436
00:28:34,620 --> 00:28:37,120
from coming in contact with each other.

437
00:28:37,220 --> 00:28:39,520
As you can imagine, the size of these is tremendous because

438
00:28:39,620 --> 00:28:42,320
there's tremendous forces involved in bringing two full

439
00:28:42,420 --> 00:28:45,720
sized tankers together that are carrying perhaps 300,000 tons

440
00:28:45,721 --> 00:28:48,960
tons of cargo each, we don't want them coming into contact

441
00:28:48,961 --> 00:28:52,620
at sea. With the fenders in place,

442
00:28:52,621 --> 00:28:55,460
the ships are moored alongside each other.

443
00:28:55,461 --> 00:28:59,500
After checking to make sure the tankers are secure, rubber hoses,

444
00:28:59,501 --> 00:29:02,860
measuring up to 90 feet in length, are transferred over to the

445
00:29:02,861 --> 00:29:06,080
lightering vessel. Typically, cargo is offloaded

446
00:29:06,081 --> 00:29:11,880
using a 250 PSI pump, which can transfer oil at a rate of 30,000

447
00:29:11,881 --> 00:29:16,880
an hour. By the 1970s, the growth of the

448
00:29:16,881 --> 00:29:21,560
tanker industry appeared limitless. But this burst of growth

449
00:29:21,561 --> 00:29:26,000
would soon be stunted by a series of supertanker explosions and

450
00:29:26,001 --> 00:29:32,960
several devastating oil spills. A fully loaded supertanker

451
00:29:32,961 --> 00:29:39,000
traveling at a speed of 16 knots needs up to 20 minutes to stop.

452
00:29:41,880 --> 00:29:45,180
The supertanker brought about a golden age in the oil tanker

453
00:29:45,181 --> 00:29:50,120
industry. But starting in 1969, a series of

454
00:29:50,121 --> 00:29:54,120
accidents placed the industry under scrutiny.

455
00:29:54,121 --> 00:29:57,320
In a very short period of time, there were three major tanker

456
00:29:57,321 --> 00:30:01,940
explosions. That led to immediate concern.

457
00:30:01,941 --> 00:30:05,140
The entire concept of the tanker was in jeopardy, as

458
00:30:05,141 --> 00:30:08,980
speculation rose that tankers might be floating time bombs,

459
00:30:08,981 --> 00:30:12,420
capable of igniting without a moment's notice.

460
00:30:12,520 --> 00:30:14,220
An investigation into the accidents

461
00:30:14,320 --> 00:30:16,420
found that each explosion had occurred

462
00:30:16,520 --> 00:30:20,320
during the washing of the vessel's cargo tanks.

463
00:30:20,420 --> 00:30:22,820
When you drain the cargo tanks of the oil,

464
00:30:22,920 --> 00:30:24,720
there's always a residual buildup

465
00:30:24,820 --> 00:30:26,540
on the inside of these tanks.

466
00:30:26,640 --> 00:30:29,940
So what we use is a high-pressure washing system.

467
00:30:30,040 --> 00:30:31,940
It's automatic within the tank.

468
00:30:32,040 --> 00:30:35,640
And when you induce oxygen through this spraying system,

469
00:30:35,740 --> 00:30:37,680
you've got a combustion potential.

470
00:30:37,681 --> 00:30:40,680
The velocity of that spray system actually picked up

471
00:30:40,780 --> 00:30:42,280
static electricity.

472
00:30:42,380 --> 00:30:45,380
When it hit the sidewall, it created a spark.

473
00:30:45,480 --> 00:30:48,520
And the vapors coming off ignited.

474
00:30:48,620 --> 00:30:50,920
And it was a tremendous tragedy.

475
00:30:51,020 --> 00:30:54,740
To fix the problem, engineers designed an inert gas system,

476
00:30:54,840 --> 00:30:56,340
or IGS.

477
00:30:56,440 --> 00:30:59,640
During cargo discharge, flue gases produced by an oil

478
00:30:59,740 --> 00:31:03,480
tanker's boilers are sent to the IGS.

479
00:31:03,580 --> 00:31:06,880
The system converts the flue gas to an inert gas,

480
00:31:06,881 --> 00:31:10,080
distributing it to the ship's cargo tanks and displacing the

481
00:31:10,081 --> 00:31:14,160
oxygen. Today, inert gas systems are

482
00:31:14,161 --> 00:31:18,160
mandatory aboard every oil tanker in operation.

483
00:31:18,161 --> 00:31:23,040
After the supertanker explosions of 1969, the industry would soon

484
00:31:23,041 --> 00:31:27,840
endure another crisis. By the mid-1970s, world demand

485
00:31:27,841 --> 00:31:31,680
for oil products began to taper off. The industry, which had greatly

486
00:31:31,681 --> 00:31:35,600
increased its tanker fleet due to the closures of the Suez Canal,

487
00:31:35,601 --> 00:31:39,740
was suddenly left with a surplus of ships.

488
00:31:39,840 --> 00:31:43,540
A lot of the ships that were built in the late 70s,

489
00:31:43,640 --> 00:31:45,640
in particular, went straight to layup.

490
00:31:45,740 --> 00:31:47,340
Several of those ships were never used.

491
00:31:47,440 --> 00:31:49,840
And in fact, within a handful of years,

492
00:31:49,940 --> 00:31:53,560
many of those ships were sent for demolition, for recycling,

493
00:31:53,660 --> 00:31:56,560
without ever having carried a cargo.

494
00:31:56,660 --> 00:31:59,160
By the late 80s, the demand for oil products

495
00:31:59,260 --> 00:32:01,560
was again on the rise.

496
00:32:01,660 --> 00:32:04,600
New tankers were being built, and the industry showed signs

497
00:32:04,601 --> 00:32:09,200
recovery. But its momentum would be short-lived,

498
00:32:09,201 --> 00:32:12,200
as an environmental disaster was about to change the industry

499
00:32:12,201 --> 00:32:17,640
forever. Since the dawn of the supertanker,

500
00:32:17,641 --> 00:32:20,740
hundreds of millions of gallons of oil have been spilled in

501
00:32:20,741 --> 00:32:27,760
oceans throughout the world. But no oil tanker spill has had

502
00:32:27,761 --> 00:32:31,500
a greater impact on the industry than the grounding of the Exxon

503
00:32:31,501 --> 00:32:38,000
the Exxon Valdez in 1989. On March 24, the single-hull

504
00:32:38,001 --> 00:32:42,940
Valdez hit a reef, fracturing its hull.

505
00:32:42,941 --> 00:32:47,240
The result was the leakage of approximately 11 million gallons

506
00:32:47,241 --> 00:32:51,460
of its cargo into a pristine environment.

507
00:32:51,461 --> 00:32:55,160
The Exxon Valdez severely damaged the local environment

508
00:32:55,161 --> 00:32:57,360
around Prince William Sound in Alaska.

509
00:32:57,361 --> 00:33:01,160
The oil then drifted to the west and continued to damage

510
00:33:01,161 --> 00:33:02,949
coastline along Alaska.

511
00:33:02,950 --> 00:33:08,160
It killed many animals outright, from birds to seals to sea otters,

512
00:33:08,161 --> 00:33:13,760
and then continued to damage fish populations and other aquatic populations.

513
00:33:13,761 --> 00:33:20,840
The Exxon Valdez oil spill was regretful, not only for Exxon itself, but for all the

514
00:33:20,841 --> 00:33:21,541
industry.

515
00:33:21,542 --> 00:33:26,880
What it taught all of us is even though we were operating as safely as possible

516
00:33:26,881 --> 00:33:28,980
with the emergency response equipment

517
00:33:28,981 --> 00:33:31,720
that we thought was adequate, we learned

518
00:33:31,721 --> 00:33:34,680
that we needed to do even more.

519
00:33:34,681 --> 00:33:37,380
After the grounding of the Valdez,

520
00:33:37,381 --> 00:33:39,420
public outcry and political pressure

521
00:33:39,421 --> 00:33:44,160
led to new legislation for tankers entering US waters.

522
00:33:44,161 --> 00:33:48,120
The Oil Pollution Act of 1990 effectively mandated

523
00:33:48,121 --> 00:33:50,380
the introduction of double hulls.

524
00:33:50,381 --> 00:33:53,300
The double hull basically provides for protection

525
00:33:53,301 --> 00:33:55,680
from collisions and groundings.

526
00:33:55,681 --> 00:33:58,620
And those incidents represent something like 50% of the

527
00:33:58,621 --> 00:34:01,180
incidents that tankers are involved with.

528
00:34:01,181 --> 00:34:04,460
So the double hull is providing immediately an enhanced level of

529
00:34:04,461 --> 00:34:06,400
protection.

530
00:34:06,401 --> 00:34:09,060
OPA-90, as it's known throughout the industry,

531
00:34:09,061 --> 00:34:12,900
requires all tankers entering US waters to be double hulled by

532
00:34:12,901 --> 00:34:17,560
2015.

533
00:34:17,561 --> 00:34:21,680
However, in Europe, the tanker spills of the Erika, and later

534
00:34:21,681 --> 00:34:25,220
the Prestige created a new set of legislation from the

535
00:34:25,221 --> 00:34:29,180
International Maritime Organization.

536
00:34:29,181 --> 00:34:32,420
The Prestige was an incident that occurred in November

537
00:34:32,421 --> 00:34:35,920
2002 off Spain.

538
00:34:35,921 --> 00:34:38,940
Unfortunately, it led to a very bad pollution, very major

539
00:34:38,941 --> 00:34:43,840
pollution of the beaches of Galicia.

540
00:34:43,841 --> 00:34:47,440
It also led to a lot of new legislation.

541
00:34:47,441 --> 00:34:52,180
It has led to an accelerated withdrawal, forced withdrawal,

542
00:34:52,181 --> 00:34:54,980
of single-hull tankers.

543
00:34:54,981 --> 00:34:57,680
The international mandate, which has not been adopted by the

544
00:34:57,681 --> 00:35:01,520
United States, requires tankers to be completely double-hulled

545
00:35:01,521 --> 00:35:04,560
by 2010.

546
00:35:04,561 --> 00:35:08,300
But the tanker owners doing business in U.S. waters have

547
00:35:08,301 --> 00:35:11,640
more immediate concerns than the double-hull deadline.

548
00:35:11,641 --> 00:35:14,340
They have to make sure their current fleet of tankers can pass

549
00:35:14,341 --> 00:35:18,340
the stringent maritime regulations enforced by the U.S. Coast Guard.

550
00:35:19,880 --> 00:35:20,580
The U.S.

551
00:35:20,581 --> 00:35:25,640
Coast Guard has the unique responsibility for being the maritime service that protects

552
00:35:25,641 --> 00:35:30,135
America's waterways, ensures safety, and commerce at the same time.

553
00:35:30,136 --> 00:35:31,880
When the Coast Guard comes

554
00:35:31,881 --> 00:35:36,380
aboard a tank ship, our concerns are to make sure that the vessels don't create a pollution

555
00:35:36,381 --> 00:35:40,760
hazard to the area. On a warm June day in Long Beach,

556
00:35:40,761 --> 00:35:44,360
British, California, the British Pride has just returned from the

557
00:35:44,361 --> 00:35:48,360
Middle East, carrying 2 million barrels of crude oil in its hull.

558
00:35:48,361 --> 00:35:51,400
Rick Hawkins, United States Coast Guard.

559
00:35:51,401 --> 00:35:54,400
This is David Nguyen and Mike Garcia.

560
00:35:54,401 --> 00:35:55,440
How are you doing today? Good morning,

561
00:35:55,441 --> 00:35:56,880
local board. I'm fine. Thank you very much.

562
00:35:56,881 --> 00:35:59,440
What we're here today to do, Captain, is conduct your annual

563
00:35:59,441 --> 00:36:04,320
certificate of compliance inspection. If major problems are

564
00:36:04,321 --> 00:36:07,720
found aboard the British Pride, the ship won't be allowed to

565
00:36:07,721 --> 00:36:12,120
charge its cargo, potentially costing the vessel's owner tens

566
00:36:12,121 --> 00:36:16,880
of thousands of dollars a day. One of the most important

567
00:36:16,881 --> 00:36:20,920
anti-pollution devices on board a tanker is the oily water

568
00:36:20,921 --> 00:36:24,920
separator. The system works by processing

569
00:36:24,921 --> 00:36:27,960
the oil that exudes from a tanker's machinery and collects

570
00:36:27,961 --> 00:36:33,160
in the lower most part of the ship, known as the bilge.

571
00:36:33,161 --> 00:36:36,820
When the oil is diluted with water to acceptable levels, it

572
00:36:36,821 --> 00:36:40,660
discharged into the ocean. There are safety systems built

573
00:36:40,661 --> 00:36:45,460
into that equipment where should the oil reach a high level, that

574
00:36:45,461 --> 00:36:48,660
alarms will sound and automatic systems come into play.

575
00:36:48,661 --> 00:36:52,260
Vowels will close, so there's no accidental discharge.

576
00:36:52,261 --> 00:36:54,500
Thank you. After examination of the

577
00:36:54,501 --> 00:36:57,540
oily water separator, the wheelhouse is tested.

578
00:36:57,541 --> 00:36:59,940
Can we go from a hard port to a hard seller?

579
00:36:59,941 --> 00:37:03,540
Half port to a hard seller? That's hard to port.

580
00:37:06,820 --> 00:37:09,380
emergency firefighting equipment is checked,

581
00:37:09,381 --> 00:37:14,220
and manual control of the ship's rudder is inspected.

582
00:37:14,221 --> 00:37:17,060
Upon completion, the vessel is given permission

583
00:37:17,061 --> 00:37:20,660
to discharge its cargo. Based on the inspection

584
00:37:20,661 --> 00:37:23,460
this morning of the Pride, the ship was in very good condition.

585
00:37:23,461 --> 00:37:26,580
The crew, the officers, and the deck crew were very competent,

586
00:37:26,581 --> 00:37:29,380
very cooperative, and all the machinery that we inspected

587
00:37:29,381 --> 00:37:32,980
was in very good condition and operated as intended.

588
00:37:32,981 --> 00:37:36,420
Monitoring the hundreds of oil tankers entering U.S. harbors,

589
00:37:36,421 --> 00:37:39,720
is a huge responsibility for the Coast Guard.

590
00:37:39,721 --> 00:37:42,060
And they're about to get even busier,

591
00:37:42,061 --> 00:37:43,720
as the tanker industry is currently

592
00:37:43,721 --> 00:37:46,720
undergoing the highest level of new ship construction

593
00:37:46,721 --> 00:37:52,720
since post-World War II. The largest oil tanker spill

594
00:37:52,721 --> 00:37:57,180
on record occurred in 1979, when two tankers collided

595
00:37:57,181 --> 00:38:01,620
off Tobago Island, spilling approximately 280,000 metric

596
00:38:01,621 --> 00:38:05,520
tons of oil into the Caribbean Sea.

597
00:38:07,420 --> 00:38:09,920
At the National Steel and Shipbuilding Company

598
00:38:10,020 --> 00:38:16,260
in San Diego, California, BP is building four new Alaskan oil

599
00:38:16,360 --> 00:38:21,460
tankers to meet the OPA-90 deadline of 2015.

600
00:38:21,560 --> 00:38:26,380
At 185,000 dead weight tons, each ship

601
00:38:26,480 --> 00:38:32,380
consists of 34,000 tons of steel and requires 106,000 gallons

602
00:38:32,480 --> 00:38:35,180
of paint.

603
00:38:35,181 --> 00:38:39,180
But what is unique about these ships is a redundancy system

604
00:38:39,280 --> 00:38:42,180
that ensures a level of safety far beyond the requirements

605
00:38:42,280 --> 00:38:43,880
of OPEN 90.

606
00:38:43,980 --> 00:38:45,420
We call it double-double.

607
00:38:45,520 --> 00:38:47,820
It's double hull, double engine, double propeller,

608
00:38:47,920 --> 00:38:49,120
double everything.

609
00:38:49,220 --> 00:38:52,940
And they're designed to enhance the safety of transporting oil

610
00:38:53,040 --> 00:38:54,240
out of Alaska waters.

611
00:38:54,340 --> 00:38:56,440
If the engine were to fail, the other one

612
00:38:56,540 --> 00:39:00,280
could operate independently and propel that ship.

613
00:39:00,380 --> 00:39:01,680
The same with the rudder.

614
00:39:01,780 --> 00:39:03,680
If you were to lock your rudder in

615
00:39:03,681 --> 00:39:05,480
in and you couldn't move that rudder.

616
00:39:05,481 --> 00:39:08,920
You could actually shut down that engine and operate off

617
00:39:08,921 --> 00:39:11,820
of a separate propulsion system.

618
00:39:11,821 --> 00:39:13,820
The rebuilding of BP's tanker fleet

619
00:39:13,821 --> 00:39:17,360
won't come cheap, as each Alaskan tanker will cost nearly

620
00:39:17,361 --> 00:39:20,240
a quarter of a billion dollars.

621
00:39:20,241 --> 00:39:24,740
But BP has found at least one way to help offset the cost.

622
00:39:24,741 --> 00:39:29,040
Oil tankers reserve several tank apartments to store seawater.

623
00:39:29,041 --> 00:39:33,680
This is used to ballast the ship after cargo is discharged.

624
00:39:33,681 --> 00:39:36,080
If you take a simple idea of an outer wall

625
00:39:36,180 --> 00:39:38,680
and an inner wall that we now call the double hull,

626
00:39:38,780 --> 00:39:40,580
you can use that space for ballast.

627
00:39:40,680 --> 00:39:43,720
When you do, you can use more of the internal tanks

628
00:39:43,820 --> 00:39:47,100
to carry more product, crude oil or gasoline or whatever

629
00:39:47,200 --> 00:39:48,300
you may be transporting.

630
00:39:48,400 --> 00:39:51,360
You actually get more efficient use of the space.

631
00:39:51,460 --> 00:39:53,660
The Alaska Frontier will be the first

632
00:39:53,760 --> 00:39:57,680
of BP's new Alaskan class oil tankers to begin service.

633
00:39:57,780 --> 00:40:00,680
I name thee Alaskan Frontier.

634
00:40:03,680 --> 00:40:08,680
When the Alaska frontier sails, it will be the safest tanker

635
00:40:08,681 --> 00:40:11,680
sailing in U.S. waters.

636
00:40:11,681 --> 00:40:14,680
However, BP isn't the only company that is undergoing a

637
00:40:14,681 --> 00:40:17,680
massive rebuilding of its tanker fleet.

638
00:40:17,681 --> 00:40:20,680
New legislation has brought about the highest level of tanker

639
00:40:20,681 --> 00:40:24,680
construction since post-World War II.

640
00:40:24,681 --> 00:40:28,680
The reason is the industry ahead of the deadline is beginning

641
00:40:28,681 --> 00:40:31,680
to build their double-haul tankers around the world.

642
00:40:31,681 --> 00:40:32,680
the world.

643
00:40:32,681 --> 00:40:39,300
By 2010, which is still five years before the requirement for all double hull, approximately

644
00:40:39,301 --> 00:40:45,220
85% of the world's oil tanker fleet will be double hulled.

645
00:40:45,221 --> 00:40:49,560
But in spite of the new stricter laws and regulations, there are critics who believe

646
00:40:49,561 --> 00:40:52,060
there are bigger issues at hand.

647
00:40:52,061 --> 00:40:57,000
Double hulling a tanker adds safety for sure, but it's not going to fix the entire

648
00:40:57,001 --> 00:40:58,000
problem.

649
00:40:58,001 --> 00:41:00,440
It's a band-aid solution in the end.

650
00:41:00,441 --> 00:41:02,840
tankers are not the problem. Oil is the problem.

651
00:41:02,841 --> 00:41:06,580
Our nation and most nations in the world are over-dependent on

652
00:41:06,581 --> 00:41:09,180
oil. We're addicted to oil.

653
00:41:09,181 --> 00:41:11,980
While renewable energies are being developed, their

654
00:41:11,981 --> 00:41:15,980
evolution remains slow, and the world's demand for oil continues

655
00:41:15,981 --> 00:41:19,700
to increase. But oil is a non-renewable

656
00:41:19,701 --> 00:41:24,240
resource, and its supply will one day be exhausted.

657
00:41:24,241 --> 00:41:27,100
Today we're talking about oil being available certainly for

658
00:41:27,101 --> 00:41:30,300
another handful of decades, 20, 30, 40, 50,

659
00:41:30,301 --> 00:41:32,140
as routinely talked about.

660
00:41:32,141 --> 00:41:35,980
So for the tanker owner, there is not a real concern today

661
00:41:35,981 --> 00:41:38,740
that the world is about to stop shipping oil.

662
00:41:38,741 --> 00:41:41,420
In parallel with the exploration of oil

663
00:41:41,421 --> 00:41:45,520
and the utilization of oil, gas is becoming a substitute,

664
00:41:45,521 --> 00:41:47,480
in many cases, for oil.

665
00:41:47,481 --> 00:41:50,760
It is the growth industry in terms of an energy source.

666
00:41:50,761 --> 00:41:54,360
It's cleaner, it burns more efficiently,

667
00:41:54,361 --> 00:41:59,360
and has a huge role to play in reducing greenhouse gases

668
00:41:59,361 --> 00:42:01,520
and emissions around the world.

669
00:42:01,521 --> 00:42:03,920
Transporting natural gas to the United States

670
00:42:03,921 --> 00:42:09,280
will be the job of the LNG, or liquid natural gas tanker.

671
00:42:09,281 --> 00:42:12,360
LNGs store the gas in liquid state

672
00:42:12,361 --> 00:42:15,960
by cooling it to a temperature of minus 256 degrees

673
00:42:15,961 --> 00:42:18,460
Fahrenheit.

674
00:42:18,461 --> 00:42:20,900
In addition to their more environmentally friendly

675
00:42:20,901 --> 00:42:25,540
LNGs are less polluting than oil tankers, using mainly natural

676
00:42:25,541 --> 00:42:29,540
gas for propulsion. And at 900 feet in length, with a

677
00:42:29,541 --> 00:42:34,340
draft of 36 feet, LNGs can enter ports that would be restricted

678
00:42:34,341 --> 00:42:39,460
to most large-sized oil tankers. While the current LNG fleet

679
00:42:39,461 --> 00:42:43,860
numbers just under 200, that number is expected to increase

680
00:42:43,861 --> 00:42:47,460
in the coming years. Some reports suggest that by

681
00:42:47,461 --> 00:42:52,760
that by 2050, there will be as many LNG carriers, 450, 500,

682
00:42:52,860 --> 00:42:57,600
as there are very large crude carriers, oil carriers today.

683
00:42:57,700 --> 00:43:01,000
Although oil tankers remain the most important energy lifeline

684
00:43:01,100 --> 00:43:04,420
to the world, the increase of the LNG fleet

685
00:43:04,520 --> 00:43:08,520
signals that its tenure of dominance is limited.

686
00:43:08,620 --> 00:43:11,720
Declining oil reserves combined with a global increase in energy

687
00:43:11,820 --> 00:43:15,060
consumption will inevitably force the proliferation

688
00:43:15,061 --> 00:43:19,000
of alternative fuels. As alternatives emerge,

689
00:43:19,001 --> 00:43:22,860
as we start to develop other ways to get around in our cars,

690
00:43:22,861 --> 00:43:25,000
hopefully tankers will become obsolete.

691
00:43:25,001 --> 00:43:27,000
Hopefully refineries will become obsolete,

692
00:43:27,001 --> 00:43:29,500
and we will get off of our oil addiction.

693
00:43:29,501 --> 00:43:32,820
But the footprint left behind by these steel giants

694
00:43:32,821 --> 00:43:35,920
will endure for generations to come.

695
00:43:35,921 --> 00:43:38,520
I think in the future, there will be a realization

696
00:43:38,521 --> 00:43:41,760
that it was indeed a marvel of the time,

697
00:43:41,761 --> 00:43:44,660
a necessity of the time. It's an industry

698
00:43:44,661 --> 00:43:48,900
that continuously improved, that used innovative techniques

699
00:43:49,000 --> 00:43:52,000
to make the movement of hydrocarbons even safer,

700
00:43:52,100 --> 00:43:55,200
that supplied much needed energy to places

701
00:43:55,300 --> 00:43:57,900
that before ships were built the way they are,

702
00:43:58,000 --> 00:44:02,020
we couldn't get the energy to them.

703
00:44:02,120 --> 00:44:04,320
The oil tanker will forever be linked

704
00:44:04,420 --> 00:44:08,220
to the spectacular growth of the petroleum industry.

705
00:44:08,320 --> 00:44:10,820
It will serve as a reminder of a time

706
00:44:10,821 --> 00:44:15,920
when nations depended on oil, from thousands of miles away

707
00:44:16,020 --> 00:44:18,320
to power the industrialized world.

708
00:44:40,820 --> 00:44:42,880
you

